        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the retailer is a lost-volume seller entitled to recover its lost profit under UCC § 2-708(2).\n\nUnder UCC § 2-708(1), the standard measure of damages for a buyer's repudiation is the difference between the market price and the unpaid contract price. However, UCC § 2-708(2) provides that if the standard measure of damages under subsection (1) is inadequate to put the seller in as good a position as performance would have done, then the measure of damages is the profit (including reasonable overhead) which the seller would have made from full performance by the buyer, together with any incidental damages. A seller is a 'lost-volume seller' if it has an unlimited or expandable supply of goods such that it had the capacity to make two sales instead of one. Because the retailer could acquire more computers from the manufacturer than it could sell, the sale to the bank was not a substitute transaction; had the law firm not breached, the retailer would have made two sales and earned two profits. Therefore, the retailer is entitled to its lost profit on the repudiated sale plus incidental damages.\n\n(A) is incorrect because it applies standard resale principles under UCC § 2-706, which are inadequate to protect a lost-volume seller.\n\n(B) is incorrect because failure to give notice of resale under UCC § 2-706 merely deprives the seller of the contract-resale price differential; it does not bar the seller from seeking lost profits under § 2-708(2).\n\n(D) is incorrect because an action for the price under UCC § 2-709 is available only if the goods cannot be resold at a reasonable price, whereas here the goods were readily resold."
