        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because an assignee's rights are subject to all defenses and claims for recoupment arising from the contract itself, regardless of when notice of assignment was given.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 336 and general assignment doctrine, an assignee acquires no greater rights than the assignor possessed and 'stands in the shoes of the assignor.' The obligor (homeowner) may assert against the assignee (banker) any defense or claim in recoupment that arises out of the very contract assigned, even if the breach or defense matures after the obligor receives notice of the assignment. Here, the construction contract was entire and required complete construction of the garage before the homeowner's duty to pay the $10,000 arose. When the contractor abandoned the job, he materially breached. Finishing the garage will cost the homeowner at least $8,000, and the homeowner's recoupment defense wipes out any value of the contractor's 25% part performance ($2,500). Because the contractor would recover nothing from the homeowner, the banker as assignee can recover nothing.\n\n(A) is incorrect because notice of assignment cuts off only unrelated set-offs that accrue after notice; defenses and recoupment arising directly out of the assigned transaction itself remain fully assertable against the assignee whenever they arise.\n\n(B) is incorrect because secured creditor priority under Article 9 of the UCC governs competing rights against the debtor's collateral, but does not override the obligor's substantive underlying contract defenses.\n\n(D) is incorrect because the timing of the contractor's default on his loan from the banker is legally irrelevant to the homeowner's right of recoupment under the construction contract."
