        exp: "(B) is the best response, because Elda is an intended third-party donee beneficiary of the valid bilateral contract between Alice and Barry.\n\nAlice and Barry entered into a valid, binding bilateral contract supported by consideration: each promised to pay $500 per month to Elda in exchange for the other's reciprocal promise to do the same. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302(1)(b), a third person is an intended beneficiary if recognition of a right to performance in the beneficiary is appropriate to effectuate the intention of the parties and the circumstances indicate that the promisee intends to give the beneficiary the benefit of the promised performance. Because the explicit purpose of Alice and Barry's agreement was to confer a direct monthly payment on their mother, Elda is an intended donee beneficiary. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 304, an intended beneficiary can directly enforce the promise against the promisor.\n\n(A) is incorrect because Barry's intent to contract was established when he signed the written agreement with Alice, not by his subsequent installment payments.\n\n(C) is incorrect because there is no legal doctrine barring an adult parent from enforcing an otherwise valid contract for her benefit against an adult child.\n\n(D) is incorrect because although the benefit to Elda was gratuitous as to her (making her a donee beneficiary), the contract between Alice and Barry was fully supported by consideration through their mutual exchange of promises."
